---
source: tests/help_snapshots.rs
---
Execute a bash script in a run that is in progress, at a moment.

The run must be ready. It can be a test run or a debugging session. The call waits until the run is ready before it runs the script.

The script executes on a fresh branch of the multiverse, so it does not interact with other work happening in the session, like fuzzing or other executing commands.

input_hash and vtime identify the moment to execute at, and the moment must be warm. Give input_hash as a string. A warm moment is a moment that is loaded into the run. Every moment generated in the run is warm: by fuzzing, and by an earlier runs_exec call, so the end moment of one call is warm for the next. A moment from another run is cold. Set source_run_id, or source_session_id, to load a moment from a different run. Loading a cold moment can take minutes and depends on the magnitude of the vtime. timeout does not count that time. Find a moment with the runs_properties, runs_search or runs_logs tool.

The script executes on the host, or in the container that container names. The tool needs tenant release 64.0 or newer; the doctor tool reports the tenant release. timeout is the number of seconds the server waits for the script to exit. Without it, the server's default applies (600 seconds as of release 64.0).

Returns one JSON object per frame of the stream, one per line. While a cold moment loads, frames {"status": "rewarming", "percent_complete": N} report the progress. An output frame has moment, output_text and source.stream: "info" for the script's standard output and "error" for its standard error. With events true, the frames are every event of the timeline, in the shape that the runs_logs tool returns. The last frame is the result: {"status": "exited", "exit_code": ..., "end_moment": ...}, or {"status": "timed_out", ...}. end_moment is the end of the new branch, to execute a follow-up script from. A non-zero exit code, a timeout, or a stream with no result frame marks the result isError, after all the frames.
